WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:07.650
Genome, DNA, chromosome, homologous,
diploid, haploid, genes, alleles, and

2
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:12.840
chromatids. The field of genetics uses
a whole bunch of words specific to this

3
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:17.990
area of biology. Are you overwhelmed?
Well, let's take a guided tour to

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.240
understand what they all mean and how
they fit together. First, the genome.

5
00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:28.640
This is all of the genetic material of
an organism. The human genome holds

6
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.470
many clues as to how we look and behave,
but there's so much information there

7
00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:37.210
that we still don't understand most of
it. It's a huge area of ongoing

8
00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:43.270
research. For most organisms, this
genetic information is made up of DNA.

9
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:49.100
DNA, or deoxyribonucleic acid, is the
storage structure for our biological

10
00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:54.520
information. It is made of smaller
units called bases. These are the

11
00:00:54.520 --> 00:01:01.080
famous A, T, C, and Gs. Three billion
of them strung together on a backbone

12
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:06.320
of sugar and phosphate. The DNA is
organized in two strands that fit

13
00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.840
together in a double helix. The genome
of humans is divided into several very

14
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.290
long DNA double helices, each of which
is called a chromosome.

15
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:21.930
The DNA of different humans are
incredibly similar, but not identical,

16
00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:27.420
except for identical twins. And these
differences make each of us unique.

17
00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:32.490
Each living organism has a
characteristic number of chromosomes.

18
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.760
Humans, for example, have twenty two
pairs of autosomal chromosomes, each of

19
00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:44.370
them unique in size and sequence, and
two types of sex chromosomes, X and Y.

20
00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:51.070
Most of our cells, apart from sperm or
eggs, have pairs of similar chromosomes.

21
00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:56.770
This makes us, overall, diploid
organisms. These pairs of chromosomes,

22
00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:01.060
which are called homologous
chromosomes, are very similar but not

23
00:02:01.148 --> 00:02:07.968
identical, as one is from mom and one
is from dad. The human sperm and egg,

24
00:02:07.978 --> 00:02:14.468
which are called gametes, are different
in that they only have one copy of each

25
00:02:14.478 --> 00:02:20.728
autosomal chromosome and carry only one
sex chromosome. Having just one copy

26
00:02:20.888 --> 00:02:22.058
makes gametes haploid.

27
00:02:23.308 --> 00:02:27.928
When a sperm fertilizes an egg, there
are now twenty three pairs of

28
00:02:27.928 --> 00:02:34.138
chromosomes, or forty six in total. The
fertilized egg then divides making the

29
00:02:34.138 --> 00:02:39.268
many cells of the human body. Now let's
look more closely at this process of

30
00:02:39.268 --> 00:02:45.268
cell division, which is also called
mitosis. As a cell prepares to divide,

31
00:02:45.278 --> 00:02:51.138
each DNA molecule is replicated,
resulting in two identical copies of

32
00:02:51.148 --> 00:02:56.748
each chromosome. This means that the
forty six chromosomes temporarily

33
00:02:56.758 --> 00:03:03.508
double to ninety two chromosomes in
each cell. The two exact copies of each

34
00:03:03.508 --> 00:03:06.058
chromosome are called sister chromatids.

35
00:03:07.178 --> 00:03:11.368
Initially, the sister chromatids are
connected to each other at a central

36
00:03:11.368 --> 00:03:17.578
point, which is often depicted as the
X-shaped drawing of a chromosome. At

37
00:03:17.578 --> 00:03:22.898
the end of mitosis, the two sister
chromatids detach and are pulled apart

38
00:03:22.898 --> 00:03:28.318
in opposite directions. Two daughter
cells are created, each with forty six

39
00:03:28.318 --> 00:03:34.358
chromosomes and the exact same genome,
So let's look closer at the structure

40
00:03:34.358 --> 00:03:40.478
of a chromosome. Within the long DNA
strand, there are regions called genes

41
00:03:40.488 --> 00:03:45.828
that contain the instructions to make
proteins. There are around twenty two

42
00:03:45.838 --> 00:03:48.708
thousand protein coding genes in humans.

43
00:03:49.958 --> 00:03:55.698
The proteins that genes code for make
us who we are, they give us ginger hair

44
00:03:55.708 --> 00:04:01.377
or be blood type O. These traits are
also called phenotypes in the field of

45
00:04:01.377 --> 00:04:02.017
genetics.

46
00:04:03.227 --> 00:04:07.187
Zooming back out to the chromosome
level once again, we can start to

47
00:04:07.197 --> 00:04:13.627
understand where variation in human
phenotype begins. As humans have pairs

48
00:04:13.637 --> 00:04:19.596
of homologous chromosomes, it follows
that they also have pairs of genes. A

49
00:04:19.596 --> 00:04:25.197
pair of genes may be identical to one
another, or they may differ subtly in

50
00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:30.777
their DNA sequences, in which case
they are called alleles. Sometimes

51
00:04:30.787 --> 00:04:36.487
different alleles still give rise to
the same phenotype. If the two genes on

52
00:04:36.487 --> 00:04:41.797
homologous chromosomes produce the same
phenotype, for example, both causing

53
00:04:41.797 --> 00:04:48.097
blue eyes, the person is homozygous for
that phenotype. However, sometimes the

54
00:04:48.097 --> 00:04:52.917
two alleles produce different
phenotypes, for example, blue eyes and

55
00:04:52.917 --> 00:04:55.517
brown eyes. This makes a person
heterozygous.

56
00:04:56.547 --> 00:05:01.147
The actual outcome in the person, for
example, what eye color they end up

57
00:05:01.157 --> 00:05:07.037
with, depends on whether one allele is
dominant over the other. For example,

58
00:05:07.127 --> 00:05:11.647
the allele for brown eyes is dominant
over blue, so the person will have

59
00:05:11.657 --> 00:05:17.647
brown eyes. The blue allele is
recessive because it has no effect on

60
00:05:17.647 --> 00:05:19.227
the phenotype of the heterozygote.

61
00:05:20.727 --> 00:05:25.477
Most phenotypes, however, are not as
simply explained and understood as eye

62
00:05:25.477 --> 00:05:30.667
color. Height, intelligence, and
susceptibility to different diseases,

63
00:05:30.667 --> 00:05:36.087
for example, are complex blends of
numerous genes and their many alleles.

64
00:05:36.547 --> 00:05:42.597
Phew! Now that we've covered all of
these different terms involving DNA, we

65
00:05:42.597 --> 00:05:48.137
can delve into all of the amazing
things that DNA does and understand how

66
00:05:48.137 --> 00:05:52.467
the study of genetics is
revolutionizing medicine and advancing

67
00:05:52.477 --> 00:05:53.837
our knowledge of life.

